{-
   An array grown past what one region of the heap holds - Native's `growHeap`.

   The allocator is a bump pointer over mapped address space with a free list per size class, and it
   used to have exactly one region of 4 MiB. That is a smaller ceiling than it sounds, because a
   doubling container does not spend those bytes once: growing to 2^k slots takes a block at every
   class below it on the way, and each is freed to a list that only ever hands blocks back at its own
   class - so the live array is the last block and roughly as much again is dead behind it. `push` on
   an `[Int]` therefore stopped at exactly 131072, with the 1 MiB block that capacity fits in and
   ~1 MiB of abandoned smaller ones leaving no room for the 2 MiB the next doubling asked for.

   And it stopped *silently*: `reserve` discarded `resize`'s answer, and `push` compared the count
   against the capacity and returned, discarding the element it had been handed. An array that
   quietly stops growing is the kind of wrong answer nothing downstream can notice, which is why the
   comparison is a `checkCondition` now - Implementation-Containers.md §7.1's "in the same trap tier
   as a bounds check", which the fixed-capacity half of `push` was already supposed to be in.

   So the number below has to be past 131072, and the loop has to *push* rather than `reserve` in one
   call: reserving the whole capacity up front takes one block and no dead ones behind it, which fit
   in the old single region and would have passed throughout.

   The elements are summed rather than counted because `length` alone does not distinguish an array
   that grew from one that grew and lost its contents to a relocation that copied the wrong extent -
   `resize` copies the old capacity rather than the live prefix, and this is the fixture that crosses
   enough size classes for that to be worth asserting.
-}

fn main() -> Int:
    let &xs = emptyArray() :: Array(Int)
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    -- 200000 elements is 800 KB of `Int` and a capacity of 262144, which is the doubling the old
    -- heap could not reach. It crosses fourteen size classes on the way and needs a second region.
    while i < 200000:
        push(xs, i)
        i = i + 1

    if (length(xs) :: Int) != 200000 then return -1

    -- Every element still where it was put, read at the boundaries the relocations landed on: the
    -- last index of the old ceiling, the first past it, and the far end.
    if xs[0] != 0 then return -2
    if xs[131071] != 131071 then return -3
    if xs[131072] != 131072 then return -4
    if xs[199999] != 199999 then return -5

    -- And the whole of it, folded, so that a copy which dropped a middle range is caught as well as
    -- one that dropped an end. Every hundredth element, because the sum of all 200000 overflows Int.
    let &total = 0 :: Int
    let &j = 0 :: Int
    while j < 200000:
        total = total + xs[j]
        j = j + 100

    -- 0 + 100 + ... + 199900 = 100 * (0 + 1 + ... + 1999) = 100 * 1999000 = 199900000
    return total
